# PART 6 — IMPROVE SYNDROME AND TIER SELECTION

Retain the existing syndrome pathways and selection functionality.

## Syndrome selector

Make the active syndrome unmistakable.

Use:

- A clear syndrome name.
- A short descriptive subtitle.
- A visible selected state.
- Consistent spacing.
- Accessible interaction feedback.

Keep the syndrome name more prominent than its numeric identifier.

Avoid long descriptions that make the navigation difficult to scan.

On mobile, use an appropriate compact selector, horizontal scrolling control, searchable selection panel, or another suitable responsive pattern.

Do not force a desktop sidebar into a narrow screen.

Do not remove existing pathways or make them inaccessible to simplify the layout.

## Tier selector

Present each available tier as a compact selection card.

Each card should show:

- Tier identifier.
- Short clinical title.
- Brief presentation description.
- Clear selected state.

Do not place the entire treatment recommendation or its supporting rationale inside the selection card.

The purpose of the selector is to identify the relevant presentation, not duplicate the complete protocol.

When a tier changes, ensure the treatment card, warnings, dosing parameters, rationale, scoring context, and handover content update appropriately.

Preserve unrelated patient assessment data when appropriate, but clear or reinitialize state only when required to prevent incorrect carryover between clinically distinct pathways.

