# PART 13 — TRIAGE, EVIDENCE, AND HANDOVER

## 13.1 Symptom triage

Retain the existing triage function.

Present each differential pattern as a separate, scannable item with:

- Key identifying features.
- The interpretation stated in the existing content.
- The relevant pathway or reference, where available.

Do not turn a suggestive clinical pattern into a definitive diagnosis unless the original content explicitly supports that interpretation.

## 13.2 Evidence library

Retain and improve the existing evidence library.

Display available information in a consistent hierarchy:

- Guideline or source name.
- Publication year.
- Study or document title.
- Study design, when available.
- Main reported finding.
- Relevant limitations.
- Link to the original source, when an actual URL exists.

Keep references easy to open and read.

Do not invent citations, publication details, source URLs, or study metadata.

Do not allow evidence content to dominate the initial treatment view.

## 13.3 Handover note

Retain the current structured handover functionality.

Ensure that the generated note remains synchronized with the selected syndrome and tier.

Where supported by the existing data, organize it into:

- Clinical presentation.
- Assessment findings.
- Recorded stabilization checks.
- Patient-context modifiers.
- Selected treatment.
- Dose, route, and timing.
- Critical safety restrictions.
- Monitoring.
- Escalation and follow-up.

Only include information supported by the application state.

Do not mark an unchecked assessment as normal.

Do not represent a proposed intervention as already administered.

Do not represent missing information as a negative finding.

Preserve the ability to copy or otherwise use the generated note, and make the final output readable in a clinical handover context.

